"A competing-consumer diagram shows multiple workers drawing work from one logical queue with explicit acknowledgment and retry behavior.\n\nUse when: Explain background processing and queue-based work distribution. Review retry, poison-message, and worker failure paths.\n\nAvoid when: Every independent consumer must receive its own copy of each event.\n\nVisual grammar: A queue denotes buffered work, with workers grouped as one logical consumer pool. Delivery arrows encode task assignment rather than broadcast. Acknowledgment, retry, and dead-letter paths use distinct labels.\n\nBuild: Define a work item and the queue’s delivery semantics. Draw producer, queue, worker pool, and result destination. Add lease or visibility timeout and failure routing if supported. Separate acknowledgment and retry paths from the main delivery arrows.\n\nCheck: One successful processing claim is not mistaken for exactly-once side effects. Worker concurrency and ordering assumptions are stated. Duplicate handling and poison-message outcomes are identifiable. Queue labels and worker text remain free of return arrows.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing several workers as if each receives every message. Treating acknowledgment as proof that an external transaction completed safely.\n\nExample: Image jobs enter a queue; any available worker processes one and acknowledges it, while repeated failures reach a dead-letter queue.\n\nAlternatives: Publish-subscribe diagram, Transactional outbox diagram."
